A user-defined package is a package created by the programmer to group his own classes, using the keyword package followed by the package name as the first statement of the file, e.g.
package mypack;
class Abc { ... }

By writing an import statement at the top of the program:
import packagename.ClassName;   e.g. import java.util.Scanner;
import packagename.*;           e.g. import java.io.*;   (imports all classes of the package)
(Classes of java.lang are imported by default.)

Yes. An object (reference variable) of another class can be declared as a data member of a class, since a class is a user-defined data type, e.g.
class Student {
    String name;   // String is another class
    Date dob;      // object of class Date as a member
}
Java also allows a class to be defined inside another class (a nested class).
